    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d946">

                <pb facs="1433" n="1407" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v16/pg1407"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">396.1 GE/7–1754</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Special Adviser to the United States
                        Delegation</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bonsal</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Deputy United States Representative and Coordinator of the
                        Delegation</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-07-17">July 17, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>De Latournelle telephoned me at 5:20
                    this afternoon to say that he and Gros and
                        Cheysson would like to call on me to
                    discuss the proposed French draft declaration to be issued by the Conference. It
                    was suggested that Mr. Phleger might also
                    be present at this meeting.</p>
                <p>After consulting with you, I telephoned de
                        Latournelle at 5:55 and stated that in view of the conversation
                    which General Smith had had with M.
                        Mendes-France this afternoon and in
                    view also of the third point of the joint French–US position paper prepared in
                    Paris on July 15, there did not seem to be any useful purpose served by further
                    US working level participation in the discussion of this declaration. I
                    suggested that if there was anything unclear in the situation it should probably
                    be discussed directly by General Smith
                    and M. Mendes-France.</p>
                <p>De Latournelle stated that he would
                    report our conversation to Mendes-France.</p>
            </div>
